\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Battery v5 (final analysis). Verdicts are statements about the \emph  {registered six-lag lookup class}. ``Detected'' means the parametric-bootstrap $p$ is at its floor ($<.004$); no series has $p$ between $.004$ and $.023$, so any level in that range gives the same verdicts. This is a conservative analysis rule adopted during the final analysis, not a preregistered significance level. Detection takes precedence over equivalence, so X-ray, which also has $U_2<0.03$, is reported as detected. ``Equivalent'' $=$ no detection and $U_2<0.03$ under both-tail calibration; ``not established'' $=$ no detection but $U_2\ge 0.03$; ``detected (exploratory)'' $=$ a detected point gain just above the practical floor whose lower bounds fall below it. The i.i.d.\ null ($p=.023$) is the selection control. $L^{\mathrm  {blk}}_{D^*}$ and $L^{\mathrm  {blk}}_{\max }$ are the one-sided 95\% moving-block lower bounds at the pre-selected lag and with lag selection repeated inside each replicate (the latter bounds the \emph  {maximal} gain over the grid). VAD's fixed-$D^*$ entry is exactly zero because at $D^*{=}1$ the $k{=}6$ policy and the exact single-sample policy reduce to the same majority rule on every fold; its selection-aware entry rounds to zero. $^{\ddagger }$Inconclusive: the calibrations disagree. The fitted-Markov calibration finds the estimator biased upward ($U_2<0$), while both moving-block lower bounds are positive and the selection-aware one ($+.040$) exceeds the practical floor, and the detection test does not fire on this short series ($p=.997$). We claim neither equivalence nor a certificate. The i.i.d.\ null's \emph  {positive} paired interval is the empirical lag-selection bias (its upper end, $\approx 0.02$, is the claim floor for paired bounds, since $D^*$ is an argmax the bootstrap treats as fixed). Bold: the one interval that dwarfs every calibration concern. Sample sizes, lag grids, selected lags and block lengths are in Table\nonbreakingspace \ref {tab:details}.}}{12}{table.caption.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:battery}{{3}{12}{Battery v5 (final analysis). Verdicts are statements about the \emph {registered six-lag lookup class}. ``Detected'' means the parametric-bootstrap $p$ is at its floor ($<.004$); no series has $p$ between $.004$ and $.023$, so any level in that range gives the same verdicts. This is a conservative analysis rule adopted during the final analysis, not a preregistered significance level. Detection takes precedence over equivalence, so X-ray, which also has $U_2<0.03$, is reported as detected. ``Equivalent'' $=$ no detection and $U_2<0.03$ under both-tail calibration; ``not established'' $=$ no detection but $U_2\ge 0.03$; ``detected (exploratory)'' $=$ a detected point gain just above the practical floor whose lower bounds fall below it. The i.i.d.\ null ($p=.023$) is the selection control. $L^{\mathrm {blk}}_{D^*}$ and $L^{\mathrm {blk}}_{\max }$ are the one-sided 95\% moving-block lower bounds at the pre-selected lag and with lag selection repeated inside each replicate (the latter bounds the \emph {maximal} gain over the grid). VAD's fixed-$D^*$ entry is exactly zero because at $D^*{=}1$ the $k{=}6$ policy and the exact single-sample policy reduce to the same majority rule on every fold; its selection-aware entry rounds to zero. $^{\ddagger }$Inconclusive: the calibrations disagree. The fitted-Markov calibration finds the estimator biased upward ($U_2<0$), while both moving-block lower bounds are positive and the selection-aware one ($+.040$) exceeds the practical floor, and the detection test does not fire on this short series ($p=.997$). We claim neither equivalence nor a certificate. The i.i.d.\ null's \emph {positive} paired interval is the empirical lag-selection bias (its upper end, $\approx 0.02$, is the claim floor for paired bounds, since $D^*$ is an argmax the bootstrap treats as fixed). Bold: the one interval that dwarfs every calibration concern. Sample sizes, lag grids, selected lags and block lengths are in Table~\ref {tab:details}}{table.caption.6}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Tightness of the information envelope on approximately balanced binary pairs (Proposition\nonbreakingspace \ref {prop:tight}). Points: per-lag ratio $\sqrt  {\hat  I/2}\,/\,\mathaccent "0362{V}_1$ of the information envelope to the exact single-sample value, both computed from the same pooled lag table, for the series with $|\hat  \pi -\tfrac  12|<.035$ (lags with $\mathaccent "0362{V}_1>.025$; the i.i.d.\ null has none). Theorem\nonbreakingspace \ref {thm:mi} applied to that table forces every point to at least one. The horizontal axis is $2\mathaccent "0362{V}_1$, which equals $|r|$ for a symmetric pair, so the curve is the symmetric limit $\sqrt  {I(r)/2}\,/\,(|r|/2)$ and the dashes its leading correction $1+r^2/12$. Points above the curve at small $\mathaccent "0362{V}_1$ are the mildly imbalanced series, whose single-sample value is pulled toward Theorem\nonbreakingspace \ref {thm:hinge}'s threshold (Fig.\nonbreakingspace \ref {fig:trichotomy}). A consistency check, not an independent test.}}{13}{figure.caption.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:tightness}{{2}{13}{Tightness of the information envelope on approximately balanced binary pairs (Proposition~\ref {prop:tight}). Points: per-lag ratio $\sqrt {\hat I/2}\,/\,\widehat V_1$ of the information envelope to the exact single-sample value, both computed from the same pooled lag table, for the series with $|\hat \pi -\tfrac 12|<.035$ (lags with $\widehat V_1>.025$; the i.i.d.\ null has none). Theorem~\ref {thm:mi} applied to that table forces every point to at least one. The horizontal axis is $2\widehat V_1$, which equals $|r|$ for a symmetric pair, so the curve is the symmetric limit $\sqrt {I(r)/2}\,/\,(|r|/2)$ and the dashes its leading correction $1+r^2/12$. Points above the curve at small $\widehat V_1$ are the mildly imbalanced series, whose single-sample value is pulled toward Theorem~\ref {thm:hinge}'s threshold (Fig.~\ref {fig:trichotomy}). A consistency check, not an independent test}{figure.caption.7}{}}
